\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{Summing the field at a fixed connection}
\date{2026-11-19}

\p{Over the commutative ring #{k}, the \vocabk{connection marginal
weight}{fdip-010E} is constant on vertex-gauge orbits of links.
The \vocabk{scalar marginal weight}{fdip-010E} is invariant under
the vertex gauges on fields. The total coefficient satisfies
##{Z=\sum_U w_{\rm link}(U)=\sum_\phi w_{\rm site}(\phi).}
If #{Z} is a unit, a link observable has normalized value
##{\langle f\rangle=Z^{-1}\sum_U w_{\rm link}(U)f(U).}
For a general observable one may first take each conditional scalar
value and then this link average whenever all of those marginal
denominators are units. The corresponding statements with links
and sites exchanged also hold.}

\proof{
\p{The permutation #{\phi\mapsto\phi^g} of the scalar configurations
and \ref{fdip-010C} give
##{w_{\rm link}(U^g)
 =\sum_\phi\Xi(U^g,\phi^g)
 =\sum_\phi\Xi(U,\phi)=w_{\rm link}(U).}
Reindexing the connections by #{U\mapsto U^g} instead gives
#{w_{\rm site}(\phi^g)=w_{\rm site}(\phi)}. Summing first one
variable and then the other visits every pair once. Each gauge orbit
of links contributes its cardinality times the common marginal weight
of its members. When the
observable depends only on #{U}, its value factors out of the inner
scalar sum. For a general observable at #{U}, multiplying its
conditional value by #{w_{\rm link}(U)} recovers the inner sum.
Summing this equality and dividing by #{Z} gives the iterated
formula. The other order follows by the same finite summation.}
}
